saltator — third-party notices
==============================

saltator itself is licensed under either of Apache License 2.0
(LICENSE-APACHE) or the MIT license (LICENSE-MIT), at your option.

This file is about the *other* code a saltator binary contains. A source
checkout of this repository contains none of it — the crates below are
fetched from crates.io at build time — so this matters when you distribute
a built binary or a container image, not when you clone the repo.

The list is the Linux dependency graph with every feature enabled, which is
the largest a saltator build gets: 316 third-party crates, of which 271 are
satisfiable by taking the MIT option alone. The remainder are named below,
because each carries a term the MIT/Apache pair does not — plus the vendored
C and C++, which no crate manifest describes at all.

Nothing here conflicts with releasing saltator under MIT OR Apache-2.0.
There is no GPL-, AGPL-, or LGPL-only dependency in the graph. Two vendored
C/C++ libraries offer GPLv2 as one arm of a dual license; in both cases the
permissive arm is elected, and §"Vendored C and C++" below says so.


Regenerating this list, and the caveat
--------------------------------------

    cargo deny check licenses          # the CI gate; policy in deny.toml
    cargo tree -e normal --all-features \
        --target x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu \
        --prefix none --format '{l}|{p}' | sort -u

This file is a human-scale summary, not a substitute for shipping license
texts. MIT and the BSD licenses require the copyright notice and permission
text to travel with a binary, and reproducing 316 of those here would go
stale on the first `cargo update`. Generate the full bundle at release time:

    cargo about generate about.hbs > THIRD-PARTY-LICENSES.html


Mozilla Public License 2.0
--------------------------

    as_variant           https://github.com/jplatte/as_variant

One crate, and it is MPL-2.0 with no alternative offered. It provides the
`as_variant!` macro, and four ruma crates depend on it: ruma-client-api,
ruma-common, ruma-events and ruma-macros. ruma-common is the one that
settles it — ruma-signatures needs it, and Ed25519 over canonical JSON is
the federation trust model — so this is in *every* saltator binary and
there is no feature that sheds it.

MPL-2.0 is file-level copyleft, and §3.3 explicitly permits combining
these files into a Larger Work distributed under other terms, which is
what saltator does. The obligation that does follow you is §3.2:
distributing in Executable Form requires that the Source Code Form of the
MPL-covered files be made available at no charge, with recipients informed
of how to get it.

saltator uses as_variant unmodified, exactly as published on crates.io.
Its Source Code Form is available from the repository above and from
crates.io, at no charge, and this notice is where recipients of a saltator
binary are informed of that. If you fork saltator and modify it, that
exemption stops applying to your build and you must publish your modified
files under MPL-2.0.


Vendored C and C++
------------------

Read this section even if you skip the rest. Everything above and below is
derived from crate metadata, which is what `cargo deny` reads. The code in
this section is **invisible to that gate**: it is C and C++ source vendored
*inside* crates whose own manifests declare something else entirely, and
cargo-deny will never look at it. `librocksdb-sys` declares
"MIT/Apache-2.0/BSD-3-Clause" — which describes the Rust binding, not the
half-million lines of C++ it compiles.

All of it enters through one dependency: `rocksdb`, whose default features
are `snappy`, `lz4`, `zstd`, `zlib`, `bzip2`. saltator takes those defaults.

    RocksDB (C++)        GPL-2.0 OR Apache-2.0     rocksdb/COPYING,
                                                   rocksdb/LICENSE.Apache
    LevelDB (C++)        BSD-3-Clause              rocksdb/LICENSE.leveldb
                                                   (RocksDB descends from it)
    snappy               BSD-3-Clause              Google
    zstd                 GPL-2.0 OR BSD-3-Clause   zstd/COPYING, zstd/LICENSE
    lz4                  BSD-2-Clause (lib/)       GPL-2.0 covers only
                                                   programs/ and examples/,
                                                   which are not compiled
    zlib                 zlib license              via libz-sys
    bzip2                BSD-style                 via bzip2-sys

**RocksDB and zstd are elected under Apache-2.0 and BSD-3-Clause
respectively.** Both are genuine dual licenses where the copyright holder
offers a choice, and taking the permissive arm is what makes saltator
distributable under MIT OR Apache-2.0. A `COPYING` file containing the
GPLv2 text is present in both source trees and means only that the other
arm exists — not that anything here is GPL'd. Record the election here
rather than leaving a reader to find GPLv2 in the build tree and draw their
own conclusion.

If the compression set is ever narrowed — a homeserver does not obviously
need five compression backends — this section shrinks with it, and so does
the build. That is a size question, not a license one.


